WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:08.670
So this topic probably doesn’t sound very
interesting, but I promise it’s at least

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:09.750
somewhat interesting.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.429
Keep in mind that everything I’m saying
here is about the most efficient and profitable

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:17.289
design of a grocery store so don’t go complaining
if your neighborhood Tesco or Safeway isn’t

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:21.180
exactly the same, but chances are next time
you go to the grocery store you’ll notice

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.369
at least some of these principles at work.

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.820
Let’s start with the entrance of the grocery
store.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.870
Americans in particular love shopping in a
counter-clockwise path.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:36.120
For this reason, the door to grocery stores
will typically be on the right hand side in

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.739
the United States and the checkout counters
will be on the left.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.120
In the UK, it’s often the opposite.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.319
For this reason, researchers speculate that
this tendency to move in one direction is

00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:49.190
linked to the side of the road that the two
demographics drive on.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.190
In one research study, Americans’ desire
to move in a counter-clockwise path in a grocery

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.190
store was so great that they actually went
around physical barriers that researchers

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.190
put up in the store in order to move in a
counterclockwise direction.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.629
This tendency actually works in stores’
favor as well.

00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:57.179
Research shows that profits are higher in
US grocery stores with an entrance to the

00:00:57.179 --> 00:00:58.179
right.

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:02.309
It allows individuals to be move in the path
that they like and also exposes shoppers to

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:04.960
the most amount of merchandise possible.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.900
In fact, Americans spend on average $2 more
per trip when they move through a grocery

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.150
store in a counter-clockwise pattern.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.371
This may not seem like much, but when considering
that every American goes to the grocery store

00:01:16.371 --> 00:01:22.390
an average of 1.5 times per week, or 78 times
a year, this amounts to enormous differences

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.020
in profits.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.580
The periphery of grocery store is also very
calculated.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.690
Items that are almost always purchased in
a trip to the grocery store—milk, meat,

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.692
eggs—are spread out around the perimeter
to force the shopper to be exposed to the

00:01:35.692 --> 00:01:38.570
most merchandise possible.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:42.380
Shoppers will almost always need to go to
these sections, so the position of the milk,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.680
meat, and eggs dictates the way a shopper
can move through the store.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.391
Within the center isles, some retailers will
put the most sold items in the center of the

00:01:51.391 --> 00:01:56.030
isles to force the shopper to walk by even
more merchandise.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:01.340
Looking at the cereal section, we can see
the principle of “eye level is buy level”.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:06.140
In the ideal circumstance, the most profitable
cereals are put at eye level.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.290
This often means that large brand name cereals
are put in these middle shelves.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.540
At a kid’s eye level, retailers will often
put the sugary kind of cereal that kids will

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.790
pester their parents for.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.469
Often on the bottom levels are the bulk price
or generic brand cereals.

00:02:22.469 --> 00:02:25.900
Those who are being frivolous are usually
willing to look around for the best deals

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.890
and often have clear intent to buy the product
ahead of time.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.620
It’s much more likely that individuals will
impulsively buy brand name cereal than bulk

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.790
size generic cereal.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.950
On the top level is usually the healthy or
small-brand cereal.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.239
Usually those who are buying healthy cereal
have clear intent to buy that cereal, and

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:47.200
profits are often lower on these cereals.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.639
Also a prime location for items that retailers
want to push is the endcaps—the ends of

00:02:51.639 --> 00:02:53.040
isles.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.010
Almost all shoppers will pass these endcaps
because of the perimeter pattern we discussed

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.090
earlier.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.540
Interestingly, the liquor store monopoly in
Sweden uses this principle in reverse.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.230
The monopoly was created by the government
to promote moderation in alcohol consumption

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:13.579
so one of the things they do to prevent impulse
purchases is not have any endcap displays.

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:17.989
So thats all the ways the layout of grocery
stores is designed to manipulate you.

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:21.290
Next time you go to the grocery store look
out for some of these retail principles and

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:32.969
I’m sure you’ll be able to spot at least
some of them.

